(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-acid-base-disorders-ii-vignettes"]={"title":"Acid-Base Disorders II Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with cirrhosis takes a diuretic, is anxious and has low albumin. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.58 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 34 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 31 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 135 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 93 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in metabolic alkalosis carbon dioxide rises 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate. Which part of her history best explains the metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["Her anxiety","Her low albumin","Her liver disease alone","Her diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate 31 with pH 7.58 is a metabolic alkalosis; expected carbon dioxide = 40 + 0.75 x 6 = 44.5, but hers is 34, so a respiratory alkalosis is also present. Diuretics are a saline-responsive cause of metabolic alkalosis, and liver disease with diuretics is the listed setting. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 53, 39"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has received large volumes of normal saline for 3 days after abdominal surgery. She also takes acetaminophen for pain, has nasogastric suction, and takes a loop diuretic for edema. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 115 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of her care should be limited?","choices":["Further large-volume normal saline","Her acetaminophen for pain","Her nasogastric suction","Her loop diuretic for edema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 140 - (15 + 115) = 10, normal, with a high chloride. Saline infusion is a normal-gap cause, so the infusion feeding the acidosis is the part to limit. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 45, 46, 53"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with self-induced vomiting has a metabolic alkalosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.51 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Her vomiting has stopped and saline has been started. Which approach to her potassium should be avoided?","choices":["Replacing well beyond the measured deficit","Replacing potassium alongside her fluids","Rechecking potassium as the alkalosis corrects","Treating the cause of her vomiting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Much of the low potassium in alkalosis is a shift into cells. Potassium deficits are treated if needed, but overcorrection is to be avoided, because as the alkalosis corrects potassium shifts back out of the cells. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 54"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is brought in confused with a low blood pressure, and the cause of his critical illness is not yet clear. Which initial testing best characterizes his acid-base status?","choices":["Urine chloride before any blood testing","Arterial gas and electrolytes drawn together","Arterial gas now, electrolytes the next day","Electrolytes alone, adding a gas if abnormal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first step is to obtain an arterial blood gas and electrolytes simultaneously, so the bicarbonate on each can be compared to verify accuracy and the anion gap can be calculated. In a critically ill patient the acid-base type can assist the diagnosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 9, 26, 56"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic respiratory acidosis from long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 68 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 35 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). A student predicts her kidneys will bring her pH back to 7.40 with time. Which statement about her compensation is accurate?","choices":["It moves pH toward normal without fully correcting it","It fully restores pH once 48 hours have passed","It works through the lungs blowing off carbon dioxide","It overshoots, leaving her slightly alkalemic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Compensation will not fully correct the pH disturbance; the underlying condition must be corrected to restore balance. Her kidneys have raised the bicarbonate to 35, which pulls the pH up toward normal, but it remains 7.33. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 34"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has had nasogastric suction for 3 days after bowel surgery. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.41 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 37 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in chronic respiratory acidosis bicarbonate rises 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide. What does his bicarbonate indicate?","choices":["A concurrent metabolic alkalosis","Appropriate chronic compensation","Appropriate acute compensation","A concurrent metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic compensation predicts bicarbonate = 25 + 3.5 x (60 - 40) / 10 = 25 + 7 = 32. His is 37, 5 above prediction, so something beyond the kidneys is adding bicarbonate: loss of gastric acid by suction. A normal pH with a high carbon dioxide also signals a mixed disorder. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 29, 34, 54"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman on dialysis for kidney failure missed a week of sessions, has vomited for 4 days and feels anxious. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.40 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 38 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 93 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, which part of her history best explains the metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["Diarrhea from her illness","Her anxiety","Her vomiting","Her kidney failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 137 - (93 + 23) = 21, change in gap = 11, but change in bicarbonate = 25 - 23 = 2: a metabolic alkalosis is holding the bicarbonate up. Vomiting loses gastric hydrochloric acid, a saline-responsive cause, and her low chloride fits. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 50, 53"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has been breathing rapidly for 30 minutes during a panic attack. She has not vomited, has no diarrhea and takes no medicines. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.54 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 28 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her potassium is 3.3 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Which process best accounts for her potassium level?","choices":["Potassium lost through the gut from vomiting","Severe depletion of total body potassium","Potassium shifted into cells as hydrogen ions left them","Potassium shifted out of cells as hydrogen ions entered"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.54 with a carbon dioxide of 28 is a respiratory alkalosis from anxiety. In alkalosis hydrogen ions shift out of cells and potassium shifts into cells, resulting in hypokalemia, with no potassium actually lost from the body. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 39"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic respiratory acidosis from kyphoscoliosis is intubated, and the ventilator lowers her carbon dioxide from 70 to 40 mm Hg within an hour. Her bicarbonate is 35 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which complication is she now at risk for?","choices":["Cardiac arrhythmias and seizures","Hyperkalemia from potassium leaving cells","Improved oxygen delivery to tissues","A worsening metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In chronic respiratory acidosis the kidneys have raised bicarbonate over days, and it falls slowly. Lowering carbon dioxide rapidly leaves that bicarbonate unopposed, and the alkalosis may cause cardiac arrhythmias, reduced cerebral perfusion and seizures. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 37, 5"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has 4 days of diarrhea and has not eaten for a week; his serum albumin 4.6 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.25 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 21 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 9 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 110 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). His bicarbonate has fallen 16 while his anion gap has risen only 8. Which value should be examined next?","choices":["The change in chloride","The urine chloride","The albumin-corrected gap","The Winter formula prediction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 137 - (110 + 9) = 18, change in gap 8; change in bicarbonate 25 - 9 = 16. When the bicarbonate falls more than the gap rises, check the change in chloride; if gap change plus chloride change accounts for the bicarbonate change, a normal-gap acidosis (diarrhea) joins the raised-gap one (starvation ketoacidosis). — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has episodes of rapid breathing and palpitations when she is anxious; her examination is otherwise normal. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 32 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Faster breathing will help clear the episodes","Daily potassium supplements prevent the episodes","She will need mechanical ventilation if they recur","Slow, mindful breathing can ease the episodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.48 with a low carbon dioxide is a mild respiratory alkalosis from anxiety. With a pH below 7.50 treatment is usually not required; treating the anxiety and mindful breathing to control the hyperventilation are the measures. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 39, 40"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with cirrhosis has an infection and low blood pressure; her serum albumin 1.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 133 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 105 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Her gap is 12 and her carbon dioxide fits the predicted compensation. Which step should come next?","choices":["Compare changes in gap and bicarbonate","Repeat the Winter formula","Correct the anion gap for albumin","Measure the urine chloride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her gap = 133 - (105 + 16) = 12 looks normal, but albumin 1.9 is 2.6 below 4.5, so the corrected gap = 12 + 2.5 x 2.6 = 18.5, raised. The gap must be corrected when albumin is abnormal, and doing so changes her diagnosis to a raised-gap acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 57, 48"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman took a large overdose of aspirin (a salicylate) 6 hours ago and is breathing rapidly. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 25 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 96 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation","Respiratory alkalosis with respiratory acidosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Respiratory alkalosis with raised-gap metabolic acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.48 with a carbon dioxide of 25 is a respiratory alkalosis. But the gap = 140 - (96 + 18) = 26, above 20, and a large gap is never produced by a respiratory disorder alone, so a raised-gap metabolic acidosis is also present. Salicylates are a listed cause of both. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 26, 23, 44, 40"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has been treated for diabetic ketoacidosis with saline and insulin for 10 hours; her ketones are clearing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.28 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 24 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 11 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 141 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 112 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Raised-gap metabolic acidosis alone","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis","Raised-gap with normal-gap metabolic acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 141 - (112 + 11) = 18, change in gap = 8; change in bicarbonate = 25 - 11 = 14. The bicarbonate fell more than the gap rose, and her chloride of 112 is high, so a normal-gap acidosis has joined the clearing ketoacidosis, the pattern listed for treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 47, 50"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman in septic shock from pneumonia was given an opioid and a benzodiazepine for agitation and is now drowsy. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.21 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 41 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 99 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Give oxygen and recheck in a day","Increase sedation to calm her breathing","Stop the sedatives and treat the sepsis","Treat the sepsis and continue sedatives"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 137 - (99 + 16) = 22, a raised-gap lactic acidosis; expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 16 + 8 = 32, range 30 to 34, and her 41 is above it, a concurrent respiratory acidosis. Both causes must be found and treated: stop the respiratory depressants and treat the infection. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 36, 28, 49, 63"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has bipolar disorder treated with lithium and type 2 diabetes treated with metformin. Routine testing shows sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 106 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L), albumin 4.4 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL), and no monoclonal protein. Which part of her history best accounts for her anion gap?","choices":["A plasma cell dyscrasia","Lithium","Metformin","A low serum albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her gap is 136 - (26 + 106) = 4, below the normal 6 to 12. A decreased gap comes from added abnormal cations such as lithium, which are not counted in the formula. Her normal albumin and absent monoclonal protein remove the other low-gap causes. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 48"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman is drowsy 2 days after hip surgery. She takes an opioid for pain, a benzodiazepine at night for anxiety, a thiazide for hypertension and an inhaled bronchodilator for asthma. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 62 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 27 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of her medicines should be stopped?","choices":["The thiazide diuretic alone","The opioid and the benzodiazepine","The thiazide and the bronchodilator","The inhaled bronchodilator alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a high carbon dioxide and a bicarbonate only slightly raised is an acute respiratory acidosis. Treatment starts with the cause and a medicine review: stop respiratory depressants, narcotics and benzodiazepines, which reduce ventilation. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 32, 36"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease takes a diuretic for leg swelling. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.42 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 64 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 40 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 89 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in chronic respiratory acidosis bicarbonate rises 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide, and in metabolic alkalosis carbon dioxide rises 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate. Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Chronic respiratory acidosis with metabolic alkalosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis with appropriate compensation","Metabolic alkalosis with appropriate compensation","Acute respiratory acidosis with metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic compensation predicts bicarbonate = 25 + 3.5 x 2.4 = about 33; hers is 40, higher. Read from the metabolic side, expected carbon dioxide = 40 + 0.75 x 15 = about 51; hers is 64, higher. Both disorders are present and the pH sits at a normal 7.42, the listed pattern for this disease on diuretics. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 24, 34, 53"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with alcoholic ketoacidosis has vomited repeatedly for 2 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.36 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 33 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 90 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, what does comparing the change in anion gap with the change in bicarbonate show?","choices":["A raised-gap acidosis alone","An added normal-gap acidosis","An added respiratory alkalosis","An added metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 136 - (90 + 18) = 28, change in gap = 18; change in bicarbonate = 25 - 18 = 7. The gap rose far more than the bicarbonate fell, so a metabolic alkalosis from vomiting is holding bicarbonate up, detectable here because the gap is high. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 27, 45, 53"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man has had profuse watery diarrhea for 4 days and is breathing deeply. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does his carbon dioxide of 30 represent?","choices":["Respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis","A primary respiratory alkalosis","Renal compensation for respiratory alkalosis","Carbon dioxide retention from hypoventilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A low pH with a low bicarbonate is a metabolic acidosis, and diarrhea is a common cause. When the cause is metabolic, compensation is respiratory: increased breathing lowers carbon dioxide, which pulls the pH toward normal without fully correcting it. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 41, 46"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with severe pneumonia is confused and tiring, with shallow respirations and cool, mottled skin. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.20 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 40 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 141 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 105 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Normal-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 141 - (105 + 15) = 21, raised. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 15 + 8 = 30.5, range about 28.5 to 32.5; his 40 is well above it, so he is not blowing off enough carbon dioxide, a concurrent respiratory acidosis. Severe pneumonia is a listed setting for this pair. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 28, 45, 49"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure, poor tissue perfusion and a low albumin is admitted. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 32 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 108 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His albumin is 2.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). What is his anion gap after correction for his albumin?","choices":["16","17.5","20","22.5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The measured gap is 140 - (16 + 108) = 16. His albumin is 4.5 - 2.9 = 1.6 g/dL below 4.5, and 1.6 x 2.5 = 4, so the corrected gap is 16 + 4 = 20, a raised gap that fits lactic acidosis from poor perfusion. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 45, 57"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has an ileal loop bladder (a ureteral diversion) after bladder cancer surgery, takes isoniazid for latent tuberculosis, and has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin. Serum ketones are negative. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 113 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Metformin","Diabetic ketoacidosis, active","The ileal loop bladder","Isoniazid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 139 - (16 + 113) = 10, normal, with a high chloride. Ureteral diversion and the ileal loop bladder are on the normal-gap list. Isoniazid and metformin raise the gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with urosepsis in the intensive care unit is breathing 30 times a minute. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.41 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 21 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 13 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 142 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 103 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). What is her anion gap?","choices":["12","16","26","39"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gap = sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate) = 142 - (103 + 13) = 142 - 116 = 26, well above 6 to 12. A raised-gap acidosis with a normal pH means a second process: her carbon dioxide of 21 is below the predicted 25.5 to 29.5, a concurrent respiratory alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 26, 28"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with 5 days of diarrhea was given a benzodiazepine overnight for agitation and is now drowsy with slow, shallow breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.21 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 36 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 115 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis","Normal-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis","Normal-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Normal-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 139 - (115 + 14) = 10, normal, with a high chloride: the normal-gap acidosis of diarrhea. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 14 + 8 = 29, range 27 to 31; his 36 is above it, a concurrent respiratory acidosis from the respiratory depressant. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 49, 46, 36"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with chronic kidney failure has fatigue. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.29 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 103 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). What is his anion gap?","choices":["11","21","25","35"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = sodium - (bicarbonate + chloride) = 138 - (14 + 103) = 138 - 117 = 21, above the normal 6 to 12. A raised gap with a low pH and low bicarbonate is a raised-gap metabolic acidosis, and renal failure is on that list. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 26, 45"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has had nasogastric suction for 4 days after bowel surgery and has dry mucous membranes. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His potassium is 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), and his urine chloride is low. Along with stopping the suction, which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Fluid restriction with a diuretic","A hydrocortisone infusion","Ventilation to lower his carbon dioxide","Saline with potassium replacement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastric suction causes a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, confirmed by the low urine chloride, and he is volume depleted. Treatment is to stop contributors such as suction and treat the fluid and potassium deficits. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman takes a thiazide for hypertension and has had 3 days of weakness. Her blood pressure is 126/74 mm Hg. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her potassium is 3.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), and her urine chloride is low. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add a loop diuretic to the thiazide therapy","Start hydrocortisone and continue the thiazide","Restrict her salt and fluid intake for now","Stop the thiazide; give fluid and potassium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.47 with a bicarbonate of 33 and a low urine chloride is a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, and diuretics are a cause. Treatment stops the contributor and treats the fluid and potassium deficits, without overcorrecting. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 51, 53, 54"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has chronic kidney failure that has worsened over months, adrenal insufficiency on hormone replacement, glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, and leg edema treated with a loop diuretic. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 103 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["The loop diuretic","His kidney failure","His adrenal insufficiency","The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 138 - (15 + 103) = 20, above 12. Renal failure, acute or chronic, is one of the big four raised-gap causes. His other conditions do not raise the gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 46"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with severe pneumonia is increasingly somnolent. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.16 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 88 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 30 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His partial pressure of oxygen is 36 mm Hg (normal 80 to 100 mm Hg). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the blood gas in 24 hours","Secure the airway and ventilate mechanically","Withhold oxygen to protect his breathing drive","Coach him in slow, mindful breathing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A carbon dioxide above 80 mm Hg with life-threatening hypoxia (partial pressure of oxygen below 40) is an acute, severe failure of carbon dioxide excretion. He needs adequate ventilation, which may mean an emergency airway and a mechanical ventilator, plus oxygen. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 32, 36"}]};
